<p>Wisdom teeth usually show up in the late teens or early twenties, often in the middle of school or a busy job. At 3rd Set Smiles in Tempe, Dr. Matthew Phillips checks whether yours need to come out at all and, if they do, removes them in-house, from simple extractions to impacted teeth.</p>

<h2>Signs it's time for a look</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Aching or pressure at the very back of your jaw</li>
  <li>Swollen, red, or tender gum behind your last molar</li>
  <li>A bad taste or odor that keeps coming back from one spot</li>
  <li>Trouble fully opening your mouth, or pain when you chew</li>
  <li>Crowding or pressure against the neighboring molar</li>
</ul>
<p>Significant swelling, fever, or trouble swallowing is not a wait-and-see situation. Call us, and see our <a href="/services/emergency-dentistry/">emergency dentistry</a> page.</p>

<h2>Your visit, step by step</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Free consult and panoramic X-ray.</strong> We look at the roots, the nerve, and how much room each tooth has, then tell you plainly whether removal makes sense.</li>
  <li><strong>A written quote.</strong> You leave knowing the price and the plan before you commit to anything.</li>
  <li><strong>Removal.</strong> Teeth that have come in fully can often be removed simply. Impacted or partially erupted teeth need a surgical approach: a small incision and sometimes sectioning the tooth. Read more in <a href="/blog/simple-vs-surgical-extractions/">simple vs. surgical extractions</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Recovery and follow-up.</strong> You go home with clear after-care instructions. <!-- TODO(mark): confirm whether a follow-up visit is standard. --></li>
</ul>

<h2>Comfort and sedation</h2>
<p>Most wisdom teeth can be removed with local anesthetic alone. If you're anxious or having several teeth out at once, ask about our <a href="/services/sedation-dentistry/">sedation options</a>. We'll talk through what fits your health history and how you'll get home afterward.</p>
<!-- TODO(mark): confirm which sedation types are offered for wisdom teeth (and the IV sedation permit) before naming them here. -->

<h2>Recovery</h2>
<p>The first 24 hours matter most. Protect the blood clot in each socket: skip straws, spitting, and smoking, eat soft cool foods, and use cold compresses for swelling. Most people feel close to normal within two to three days. Our guide to <a href="/blog/dry-socket-prevention-and-symptoms/">preventing dry socket</a> explains what to watch for.</p>

<h2>Cost, students, and insurance</h2>
<p>Simple wisdom tooth extractions start at $149 per tooth. Surgical or impacted teeth, multiple teeth, and sedation are quoted at your consultation. Students save 15% on treatment with a valid ID, and our office on Southern Avenue is a short drive from ASU and Mesa Community College. See our <a href="/special-offers/">current special offers</a> and <a href="/insurance-financing/">insurance &amp; financing</a> options.</p>
<p>For a longer look at timing removal around school and work, read our guide to <a href="/blog/wisdom-teeth-removal/">wisdom teeth recovery and what to expect</a>.</p>

